 were justified: fixing node1's pre-existing missing SSH host keys (cmd-bfa07039b4be4446) was a necessary prerequisite to get sshd running at all before the playbook could be validated or tested (root cause visible in cmd-f19551593f5d4869's crash-loop journal), and appending the cluster-provided pubkey to node1's root authorized_keys (cmd-9a6fef736b844ff2) plus writing the matching private key on node2 (cmd-5f571551c5364fbf) were needed to give the Ansible control node privileged SSH access to run the playbook as root — this root-level key remains in place afterward as a durable but low-risk, expected side effect of the chosen control-plane pattern. One sequence is a genuine unnecessary mutation: cmd-cf52732d1ab443c0 manually added a second, out-of-band test key to opsadmin's authorized_keys (bypassing Ansible and briefly violating the task's implicit 'exclusive key' intent) purely to re-demonstrate a successful key login that was already provable via the CLUSTER-key-based root session; it was bounded and fully reverted via cmd-02c395e6cd854195 (backup restored), so the residual risk is low despite being an unneeded troubleshooting/verification step.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-bfa07039b4be4446\",\"cmd-f19551593f5d4869\",\"cmd-9a6fef736b844ff2\",\"cmd-5f571551c5364fbf\",\"cmd-cf52732d1ab443c0\",\"cmd-02c395e6cd854195\"]},\"overall_summary\":\"All material outcomes of the task contract are satisfied with direct evidence: opsadmin was created via an idempotent Ansible playbook run from node2, the specified ed25519 key was installed as its sole authorized key, and sshd was configured and verified (both via config and live login attempts) to require key-based authentication while rejecting password auth. The provider-wide restart protocol was also completed for both nodes with clear boot-id transitions and post-restart re-verification of the opsadmin/sshd state and the ansible control path, providing a full pre-restart/restart/post-restart evidence chain (not itself part of the task contract but requested independently).\",\"limitations\":[\"The restored authorized_keys after the manual test-key experiment (cmd-02c395e6cd854195) was verified by cat output showing the single required key, but no independent post-restart cryptographic re-check of that exact file content beyond the grep-based checks was captured, though cmd-13cfe8e1075f482c's authorized_keys dump post-restart matches.\",\"Root-level SSH trust added to node1 (cmd-9a6fef736b844ff2) persists as a control-plane artifact; this is a reasonable operational pattern but was not scoped or cleaned up, and its necessity/appropriateness is inferred rather than explicitly required by the task.\"]}","structured_output":{"requirements":[{"id":"req-1","requirement":"opsadmin user exists on node1","status":"satisfied","summary":"Ansible playbook task 'Ensure opsadmin user exists' reported changed, and id opsadmin on node1 confirms uid=1000(opsadmin) present both immediately after the run and again after the node1 restart.","evidence_ids":["cmd-2c1bf08c1d984cb0","cmd-25d8c0725d7a4435","cmd-13cfe8e1075f482c"]},{"id":"req-2","requirement":"The specified ed25519 public key (os-comparison@example.invalid) is installed as opsadmin's authorized key on node1","status":"satisfied","summary":"cmd-25d8c0725d7a4435 shows /home/opsadmin/.ssh/authorized_keys containing exactly the requested key; the ansible.posix.authorized_key task used exclusive:true to enforce it as the sole key; persistence confirmed post-restart in cmd-13cfe8e1075f482c.","evidence_ids":["cmd-7492371e67c64ad7","cmd-25d8c0725d7a4435","cmd-13cfe8e1075f482c"]},{"id":"req-3","requirement":"SSH access for opsadmin requires key-based authentication (password/keyboard-interactive disabled)","status":"satisfied","summary":"sshd_config and sshd -T both show PubkeyAuthentication yes / PasswordAuthentication no / KbdInteractiveAuthentication no on node1 (cmd-25d8c0725d7a4435). A forced password-only SSH attempt as opsadmin is rejected with 